The number of shares of our Class A common stock, Class N common stock, Class T common stock, and Class F common stock that will be outstanding after this offering and the concurrent private placement is based on 6,626,640,437 shares of our Class A common stock, 5,307,329,452 shares of our Class N common stock, one share of our Class T common stock, and one share of our Class F common stock outstanding as of September 30, 2026, after giving effect to:
•the automatic conversion of (i) all 438,406,253 outstanding shares of our Series A, C-1, D-1, D-2, E-1, F-1, G-1, and H-1 redeemable convertible preferred stock into 4,408,097,245 shares of Class A common stock and (ii) all 414,987,790 outstanding shares of our Series B, C-2, D-3, E-2, E-3, E-4, E-5, F-2, G-2, G-3, G-4, H-2, H-3, and H-4 redeemable convertible preferred stock into 4,153,017,914 shares of Class N common stock, immediately prior to the completion of this offering pursuant to the terms of our amended and restated certificate of incorporation (collectively, the “Preferred Stock Conversion”);
•one share of our Class F common stock that will be issued immediately prior to the completion of this offering to the Founder LLC (the “Class F Stock Issuance”);
•the conversion of $579.3 million in aggregate principal amount of our outstanding convertible promissory notes into 276,827,518 shares of Class N common stock immediately prior to the completion of this offering, representing the maximum number of shares issuable to Amazon Web Services, Inc. upon the completion of this offering without causing it to exceed the applicable ownership cap, calculated based on the Note Capitalization (as defined below) (the “Convertible Notes Conversion”);
•the Initial Public Benefit Issuance, the shares of which (i) are considered legally outstanding, (ii) are not considered outstanding for accounting purposes while held by our wholly owned subsidiary, and (iii) accordingly will not be reflected as outstanding in our consolidated financial statements; and

•the net issuance of 49,754,965 shares of Class A common stock subject to RSUs outstanding as of September 30, 2026, for which the service-based vesting condition will be satisfied as of November 19, 2026 and the performance-based vesting condition will be satisfied upon the effectiveness of the registration statement of which this prospectus forms a part, after giving effect to the withholding of 51,578,975 shares of Class A common stock subject to such RSUs to satisfy the associated estimated tax withholding and remittance obligations (based upon the assumed initial public offering price of $74.00 per share, which is the midpoint of the estimated offering price range set forth on the cover page of this prospectus, and an assumed 50.90% tax withholding rate) (the “RSU Settlement”).
The shares of our Class A common stock, Class N common stock, Class T common stock, and Class F common stock outstanding as of September 30, 2026 exclude the following:
•1,774,342,693 shares of our Class A common stock issuable upon the exercise of options to purchase shares of our Class A common stock outstanding as of September 30, 2026, with a weighted-average exercise price of $2.91 per share;
•1,026,660,010 shares of our Class A common stock issuable upon the vesting and settlement of RSUs outstanding as of September 30, 2026, but for which (i) the service-based vesting condition will not be satisfied as of November 19, 2026 and (ii) the performance-based vesting condition, if applicable, will be satisfied upon the effectiveness of the registration statement of which this prospectus forms a part;
•47,041,287 shares of our Class A common stock issuable upon the vesting and settlement of RSUs granted after September 30, 2026;
•561,963 shares of our Class A common stock and 848,882 shares of our Class N common stock issued after September 30, 2026 in connection with our acquisitions of privately-held companies;

•1,920,580,692 shares of our Class A common stock to be reserved for future issuance under our 2026 Equity Incentive Plan (our “2026 Plan”), which will become effective prior to the completion of this offering, plus up to 3,401,163,438 additional shares of our Class A common stock, with the actual number of additional shares reserved for future issuance under our 2026 Plan equal to the sum of: (i) effective as of the effective date of the 2026 Plan, the number of shares of our Class A common stock available for issuance pursuant to our Amended and Restated 2021 Stock Plan (our “2021 Plan”) immediately prior to the termination of our 2021 Plan, and (ii) as and when shares subject to awards previously granted under our 2021 Plan are forfeited, cancelled, or would have otherwise returned to our 2021 Plan had our 2021 Plan not terminated, the number of shares of our Class A common stock subject to such awards that, after the effective date of our 2026 Plan, would have returned to our 2021 Plan and become available for issuance pursuant to future awards under our 2021 Plan had our 2021 Plan not terminated;
•128,038,713 shares of our Class A common stock to be reserved for future issuance under our 2026 Employee Stock Purchase Plan (our “ESPP”), which will become effective prior to the completion of this offering;
•933,698,210 shares of our Class N common stock reserved for future issuance under our Donation Matching Plan (our “Donation Matching Plan”) as of September 30, 2026, which will be amended and restated prior to the completion of this offering to provide that the maximum number of shares of Class N common stock reserved for future issuance shall equal: (i) 128,038,713 shares of our Class N common stock plus (ii) the number of shares of our Class N common stock equal to the total shares of Class N common stock available for issuance pursuant to our Donation Matching Plan immediately prior to the completion of this offering; of which 553,441,297 shares represent the maximum number of shares issuable as matching contributions on employee donations that may be made based on employee pledges on vested equity awards outstanding as of September 30, 2026, less the Matching Issuance (as defined below) (such total, the “Vested Matching Obligation”); and

Our success depends in large part on our ability to continue to develop the most advanced and capable AI models, and any failure to do so could adversely affect our business.
Our success depends in large part on our ability to continuously develop, improve, and deploy the most advanced, capable, and useful AI models. Achieving and maintaining this position depends on our ability to:
•advance the frontier of AI capabilities through breakthrough research, innovative architectures, and efficient training methodologies;
•ensure our models are safe, reliable, and trustworthy while maintaining competitive performance;
•secure the compute and infrastructure necessary to power and support our increasing needs;
•advance compute efficiency through algorithmic and architectural innovations, including compute multiplier advancements;
•retain the continued service of our co-founders, senior management team, and key technical employees, and attract, retain, and motivate leading researchers, engineers, and technical talent in an intensely competitive and evolving market;
•continue revenue growth and maintain access to sufficient capital, including through potential equity and debt financing, to make the investments we believe are necessary to support our growth trajectory;
•secure and maintain relationships with data providers, licensors, and partners to obtain the diverse, high-quality datasets necessary for world-class model performance;
•secure and maintain relationships with cloud, ecosystem, and other partners to facilitate the diffusion of Claude;
•build, operate, and maintain the infrastructure required to deploy frontier models cost-effectively and at scale;
•translate model capabilities into products that meet evolving customer needs and achieve meaningful adoption;
•allocate substantial financial resources efficiently across research, infrastructure, and operations; and
•navigate an evolving, uncertain, and at times divergent global regulatory landscape and engage with legislators, regulators, and other stakeholders to promote the development of regulatory frameworks that we believe are good for society, our customers, and the long-term health of the global economy.

Our company culture has contributed to our success and, if we cannot maintain this culture as we grow, our business could be adversely affected.
We believe that our company culture is a critical component of our success. We are a mission-driven company and our culture reflects a strong emphasis on the Company Purpose. Our ability to continue to cultivate and maintain this culture is essential to our growth and continued success. We face a number of challenges associated with our commitment to our company culture, including:
•the need to identify, attract, reward, and retain people in leadership positions in our organization who share and further our culture, values, and mission;
•the increasing size and geographic diversity of our workforce, and our ability to promote a uniform and consistent culture across all of our offices and employees;
•internal and external perceptions about our mission, values, commitment to safety, and uses of our models, platform, and applications;
•engagement with government policymakers on issues that resonate differently across our workforce;
•competitive pressures to move in directions that may divert us from our mission and values;
•the rigorous, values-driven standards we apply in hiring and decision-making, which may slow the pace at which we can scale our workforce or expand our operations relative to competitors;
•the continued challenges of a rapidly evolving industry;
•the increased scrutiny and expectations associated with operating as a public company; and
•the need to balance our long-term, mission-driven objectives with shorter-term financial performance expectations, which may create internal tension and challenge our ability to consistently reinforce our cultural values and mission across teams and leadership.
Our mission-driven culture is one of our core characteristics that helps us to attract and retain key personnel and differentiates us in the market. If we are unable to maintain our culture, as well as balance our commitment to maintaining our culture with the demands of rapid growth, competition, and operating as a public company, our employee morale, engagement, and retention could suffer, requiring us to incur additional costs or adopt alternative recruiting strategies, which could adversely affect our business, financial condition, and results of operations.

If we fail to attract new customers, retain existing customers, or expand usage of our models, platform, and applications, our business, financial condition, and results of operations could be adversely affected.
Our future growth depends on our ability to attract new customers, retain existing customers, and expand sales within our existing customer base. We face significant challenges in each of these areas, and failure to execute on our customer acquisition and retention strategies could adversely affect our business, financial condition, and results of operations.
We must continue to attract new customers in a cost-effective manner. Potential customers may not understand the benefits of our models, platform, and applications or may be unable to justify the investment in our models, platform, and applications, particularly as existing competitors continue to enhance their offerings, improve performance, expand functionality, or reduce costs. Our ability to demonstrate differentiated value to potential customers may become more difficult as this competition intensifies, or as large technology companies bundle AI capabilities into existing products. If we continue to focus on enterprise customers, we may face longer sales cycles that demand significant upfront investment in enterprise sales teams and customer service. These sales efforts are costly and may not result in customer conversion.
Even if we successfully attract new customers, we face significant challenges in retaining them. Many of our customer contracts allow customers to reduce usage or terminate with limited or no notice. Customers are not obligated to renew their subscriptions or contracts with us. Customer retention and usage levels may decline due to a number of factors, including:
•dissatisfaction with the performance, reliability, safety, or accuracy of our AI models;
•availability of competitive alternatives;
•customers developing their own AI capabilities internally;
•our models, platform, and applications reducing demand for our customers’ offerings or causing disruptions to their businesses, which could lead to reduced usage or customer attrition;
•changes in customer budgets or strategic priorities regarding AI investment;
•concerns about data privacy, security, or our data handling practices;
•negative publicity regarding AI generally or our business specifically;
•changes in the regulatory and legal landscape surrounding AI and our models, platform, and applications in particular;
•pricing changes or perception that our pricing is not competitive; and
•general economic conditions or industry-specific downturns affecting our customers.

In addition, a significant portion of our revenue and accounts receivable is associated with transactions conducted with a limited number of CSPs that act as distribution and billing channels for our models. During the years ended December 31, 2023, 2024, and 2025 and the nine months ended September 30, 2026, revenue generated from sales to customers through CSP marketplaces represented approximately 11%, 32%, 47%, and 44%, respectively, of total revenue. As of December 31, 2024 and 2025 and September 30, 2026, net accounts receivable from sales through CSP marketplaces to customers represented 42%, 60%, and 52%, respectively, of our net accounts receivable. These platforms bill end customers, collect payments on our behalf, and remit such amounts to us, net of applicable platform and other fees. Any change to, or termination of, one of our significant distribution relationships could adversely affect our business by disrupting customer access to our models, reducing usage, or increasing customer acquisition, onboarding, and migration costs. In these third-party arrangements, we generally maintain separate contractual arrangements with end customers governing access to, and pricing for, our models, independent of the applicable platform, and changes in, or termination of, a particular distribution relationship would not independently eliminate those contractual relationships. However, customers may be unwilling or unable to transition to alternative access points on commercially acceptable terms or timelines, or at all, following a disruption, which could result in revenue loss, increased costs, or adverse impacts to our growth, margins, and results of operations. In addition, any delay, dispute, or operational issue affecting a platform’s collection or remittance of customer payments could adversely affect our cash flows and working capital.
We may not be able to maintain or increase revenue from our top customers for a variety of reasons, including but not limited to the following:
•customers may develop their own solutions that compete with our models, platform, and applications;
•some of our customers may redesign their systems to require fewer of our services with limited notice to us and may choose not to renew or increase their purchases of our models, platform, and applications;
•changes to our models, platform, and applications may not align with the needs or expectations of our customers and such changes may reduce customer trust in us;

We are also subject to variable interest rate risk because the interest rate under the A&R Credit Agreement varies based on a margin over an indexed rate or an adjusted base rate. If interest rates associated with our floating rate debt increase, our debt service obligations in connection with the A&R Credit Agreement would increase even though the amount borrowed remained the same, and our available cash would correspondingly decrease. Our substantial amount of debt, combined with variable interest rates, could limit our flexibility in planning for, or reacting to, changes in our business and the markets in which we operate, including limiting our future investments or our ability to enter into acquisitions and strategic partnerships, and obtain financing for such transactions.
In addition, our indebtedness, combined with our other financial obligations and contractual commitments, could have other important consequences. For example, they could:
•make us more vulnerable to adverse changes in general U.S. and non-U.S. economic, industry, and competitive conditions and adverse changes in government regulation;
•limit our flexibility in planning for, or reacting to, changes in our business and our industry;
•place us at a disadvantage compared to our competitors that have less or fixed rate indebtedness;
•limit our ability to borrow additional amounts to fund acquisitions, for working capital, and for other general corporate purposes; and
•make an acquisition of our company less attractive or more difficult.
Any of these factors could adversely affect our business, financial condition, and results of operations. In addition, if we incur additional indebtedness, the risks related to our business and our ability to service or repay our indebtedness would increase.

We have a limited operating history in an evolving and nascent industry, which makes it difficult to evaluate our future prospects and may increase the risk that we will not be successful.
We were founded in 2021 and launched our first commercial product, Claude, publicly in the first quarter of 2023. Since then, our business has grown rapidly. We operate in an industry characterized by rapid and unpredictable technological change, evolving safety and regulatory expectations, significant capital and compute requirements, and uncertain commercial adoption patterns. Our limited operating history and our evolving and nascent business and industry make it difficult to evaluate our future prospects and the risks and challenges we may encounter. These risks and challenges include our ability to:
•develop and release new models and products on a timely and cost-effective basis;
•accurately forecast our revenue, operating expenses, and compute requirements in an environment characterized by rapid technological change and usage patterns that may scale in nonlinear or exponential ways;
•secure access to sufficient compute to meet the needs of all our workloads and manage the risks related to securing compute;
•effectively manage our growth, including our cash flows and capital needs;
•accurately predict customer demands, expectations, or requirements;
•maintain and enhance our brand and reputation;
•attract, retain, utilize, and motivate our highly skilled personnel;
•build and maintain our strategic partnerships;
•anticipate and respond to macroeconomic changes and changes in the industry and markets in which we operate;
•anticipate and respond to evolving regulatory requirements and political dynamics;
•maintain our safety-focused approach while meeting market demands;
•successfully monetize our models, platform, and applications; and
•respond effectively to competitive threats.
If we fail to effectively address the risks and challenges we face, including those described elsewhere in this “Risk Factors” section, our business, financial condition, and results of operations could be adversely affected. We have encountered in the past, and will encounter in the future, risks and uncertainties frequently experienced by growing companies with limited operating histories in rapidly changing industries. If our assumptions regarding these risks and uncertainties, which we use to plan and operate our business, are incorrect or change, or if we do not address these risks successfully, our results of operations could differ materially from our expectations and our business, financial condition, and results of operations could be adversely affected.

We are at an early stage of our revenue growth, we have experienced rapid growth in recent periods, and our historical growth rates are not indicative of future performance or our ability to sustain or forecast future revenue growth.
We have grown rapidly over the last several years, and therefore our recent revenue growth and financial performance should not be considered indicative of our future performance. In 2023, 2024, and 2025, our revenue was $41 million, $386 million, and $4.6 billion, respectively, representing year-over-year growth of 841% in 2024 and 1,088% in 2025. In the nine months ended September 30, 2026, our revenue was $33.2 billion, representing a year-over-year growth rate of 1,206%. We do not expect our revenue will continue to grow at comparable rates, and we cannot assure you that it will continue to grow at all. Our recent growth may reflect early-stage adoption dynamics, the nascency of the AI market, and rapid initial customer expansion that may not be sustainable over time, and it may decline significantly and more rapidly than we anticipate, as our revenue base increases, as the market matures, and as a result of the other factors described below. We expect our revenue growth rate to decline over time. In addition, because the AI industry is very nascent, we have struggled to accurately forecast our revenue in the past and may struggle to do so in the future.
We may experience declines in our revenue growth rate as a result of a number of factors, including:
•the slowing or shifting demand for our models, platform, and applications or the market as a whole;
•our inability to secure sufficient compute and direct it efficiently across our organization;
•insufficient growth in the number of users and paying customers of our models, platform, and applications or the failure to increase sales of our models, platform, and applications to existing customers;
•failures, errors, or performance limitations in our models, including unexpected behavior, degraded outputs, outages, or inability to meet customer expectations or use-case requirements;
•increasing competition among existing competitors and new market entrants;
•the safety and risk mitigation measures we implement in developing and releasing our models, including any resulting delays in model development or release, or the adoption of laws, regulations, or standards that affect how and when frontier models may be developed or released;
•our failure to continue to capitalize on growth opportunities;

•our ability to collect amounts owed to us from customers, including as a result of customer credit risk, billing disputes, or delays in payment;
•our reliance on, and the terms of, relationships with key partners and distribution channels, and any changes to, or termination of, such arrangements;
•new or more restrictive laws, regulations, or regulatory actions affecting AI development, deployment, or use, including taxes, fees, or other measures specifically applicable to AI companies or AI-related labor displacement; and
•reputational harm, adverse publicity, or heightened scrutiny that reduces customer adoption or expansion.
We expect to continue to make investments in the development and expansion of our business, which may not result in increased revenue or growth. If our revenue or revenue growth rate declines, or our revenue growth rate declines with respect to certain markets or customer categories, our business, results of operations, and investors’ perceptions of our business could be adversely affected, which could in turn negatively impact the market price of our Class A common stock.

We have a history of net losses and may not generate sufficient revenue to achieve or sustain profitability in the future.
We have incurred net losses in each year since our founding, including net losses of $1.5 billion, $8.3 billion, and $42.0 billion for the years ended December 31, 2023, 2024, and 2025, respectively, and $104.0 billion for the nine months ended September 30, 2026, and we may not achieve or sustain profitability in the future. These net losses included changes in fair value of convertible notes, derivative liabilities, and SAFEs of $1.0 billion, $5.4 billion, and $34.3 billion for the years ended December 31, 2023, 2024, and 2025, respectively, and $94.3 billion for the nine months ended September 30, 2026, primarily driven by an increase in the fair value of our outstanding convertible promissory notes resulting from an increase in our enterprise valuation during each of the respective periods. While we have experienced significant revenue growth in recent periods, our business is highly capital intensive, and our ability to remain at the frontier of AI capabilities and secure sufficient compute depends in meaningful part on our ability to generate and sustain substantial revenue and improve our operating efficiencies. Further, our revenue growth may not be sustainable, and our rate of growth may decrease. We expect our costs will increase over time and our losses will continue as we expect to invest significant resources towards growing our business and operating as a public company.
We have expended and expect to continue to expend substantial financial and other resources related to the growth of our business, including:
•investments to enhance our access and ability to secure compute;
•continued investments in research and development to remain at the frontier of AI model development and introduce new features and enhancements to our models, platform, and applications;
•expansion of our sales and marketing activities;
•hiring additional employees;
•expansion of our operations across our multiple geographies;
•investments in our expanding general and administrative organization;
•costs associated with complying with existing and evolving laws, regulations, and industry standards applicable to AI, data protection, privacy, and security, among other things;
•investments in AI safety, risk management, monitoring, evaluation, and mitigation efforts;
•additional expenses associated with operating as a public company; and
•costs related to legal proceedings.

•the level of demand for our models, platform, and applications, and our ability to forecast such demand;
•our ability to forecast required compute and to obtain additional compute as needed, and any increase in costs thereof;
•our ability to expand usage within organizations;
•our ability to attract and retain customers and increase sales of our models, platform, and applications to existing customers;
•our ability to effectively manage our relationships with our third-party service providers and our reliance on such providers for critical infrastructure and services;
•the timing and success of new features, integrations, capabilities, and enhancements by us to our models, platform, and applications, or by our competitors to their products;
•the safety and risk mitigation measures we implement in developing and releasing our models, including any resulting delays in model development or release, or the adoption of laws, regulations, or standards that affect how and when frontier models may be developed or released;
•our ability to achieve widespread acceptance and use of our models, platform, and applications;
•changes in the competitive landscape and introduction of competitive companies, models, platforms, and applications;
•security breaches, technical difficulties, or interruptions to our models, platform, or applications;
•real or perceived improper or unauthorized use of, disclosure of, or access to confidential, proprietary, personal, or sensitive data;
•pricing pressure as a result of competition or otherwise;
•adverse litigation judgments, other dispute-related settlement payments, or other litigation-related costs;
•the number of employees hired;
•the timing of the grant or vesting of equity awards to employees, directors, or consultants;
•the vesting and settlement of equity awards, including any cash used to remit taxes on behalf of employees in connection with the settlement of RSUs and the recognition of stock-based compensation expense;
•our ability to maintain, protect, and enhance our brand;
•declines in the values of foreign currencies relative to the U.S. dollar;
•changes in, and continuing uncertainty in relation to, the legislative or regulatory environment with respect to our industry;
•enforcement by government regulators, including investigations, proceedings, fines, orders, designations, or consent decrees;
•increased legal and regulatory compliance costs in new and existing markets;
•costs and timing of expenses related to the potential acquisition of businesses, talent, technologies, or intellectual property, including potentially significant amortization costs and possible write-downs;
•catastrophic events, and resulting interruptions in the availability of our models, platform, and applications; and

Increasingly restrictive AI regulation in the United States and other Western jurisdictions could enable competitors operating in less regulated environments to innovate, scale, and deploy AI technologies more rapidly, potentially allowing such competitors to become more advanced and pose greater competitive or strategic threats over time. In particular, increasing strategic competition between the United States and China in AI development may lead to divergent regulatory approaches, with the potential for the United States to impose restrictions on AI companies’ interactions with Chinese entities or markets, and for China to impose reciprocal measures that limit access to or demand for U.S.-origin AI models, platforms, and applications.
As a result of these and other regulatory developments, our business could be subject to a wide range of regulatory limitations, obligations, or restrictions, including, among other things:
•limitations, conditions, or prohibitions on certain AI use cases, applications, or deployment contexts;
•requirements to modify, restrict, delay, or withdraw specific models, features, or functionalities in particular markets or for specific customers; for example, in early 2026, the U.S. Department of War suggested it could invoke the Defense Production Act to compel us to remove the limitations we have placed on the use of our models for autonomous weapons and mass surveillance;
•heightened transparency, documentation, auditing, governance, or human-oversight obligations;
•restrictions on the collection, use, transfer, localization, or retention of data used to train or operate AI systems, based on intellectual property, privacy, or other frameworks;
•mandated safety, risk-management, or evaluation frameworks, certifications, or approvals, including requirements for independent, third-party review or approval prior to model development, training, or release; for example, in September 2026, a state attorney general asked a court, in pending litigation alleging harm to minors, to enjoin an AI company from developing new AI models without independent, third-party guardrails and approval;
•restrictions on data center development or expansion, including permitting, zoning, or limits on energy or water usage;
•heightened energy efficiency, sustainability, or environmental compliance requirements;

Our sales to government entities and highly regulated organizations are subject to challenges and risks.
Our customers include U.S. and non-U.S. government agencies. Revenue from government agency contracts represented, in aggregate, less than 2% of our revenue in each of the periods presented in this prospectus; however, the risks described below, including the governmental actions described in this risk factor, are not limited to revenue generated directly from government agencies and could affect our relationships with commercial customers and partners. Sales to government agencies involve additional risks, including, but not limited to:
•changes in government fiscal or procurement policies, or decreases in government funding available for procurement of goods and services generally, or for our federal government contracts specifically;
•changes in government programs or applicable requirements;
•our ability to agree on commercial terms with government entities, including with respect to the permitted use of our models, platform, and applications;
•restrictions in the grant of personnel security clearances to our employees;
•our ability to maintain facility clearances required to perform on classified contracts for U.S. government and non-U.S. government agencies, as applicable;
•changes in the political environment, including before or after a change to the leadership within the government administration, and any resulting uncertainty or changes in policy or priorities and resultant funding;
•changes in the government’s attitude towards us as a company or our technology;
•appeals, disputes, or litigation relating to government procurement, including but not limited to bid protests by unsuccessful bidders on potential or actual awards of contracts to us or our partners by the government;
•the adoption of new laws or regulations or changes to existing laws or regulations;
•budgetary constraints, reductions in government spending, operating under continuing resolutions, disruptions from government shutdowns, or similar measures and constraints imposed by any lapses in appropriations for the federal government or certain of its departments and agencies;
•influence by, or competition from, third parties with respect to pending, new, or existing contracts with government customers;
•changes in legal obligations or political or social attitudes with respect to security or privacy issues;
•potential delays or changes in the government appropriations or procurement processes, including as a result of events such as war, incidents of terrorism, natural disasters, and public health concerns; and
•increased or unexpected costs or unanticipated delays caused by other factors outside of our control.

If we are unable to make acquisitions and investments, or successfully integrate them into our business, our business, financial condition, and results of operations could be adversely affected.
As part of our business strategy, we will continue to consider a wide array of potential strategic transactions, including acquisitions of businesses, new technologies, services, and other assets and strategic investments that complement our business. We have previously acquired and continue to evaluate targets that operate in relatively nascent markets, and there is no assurance that such acquired businesses will be successfully integrated into our business or generate substantial revenue. In addition, we may overpay for acquisition targets or make investments at valuations that do not ultimately prove justified by the targets’ performance, strategic value, or market conditions, which could adversely affect our financial condition.
We may be unable to identify or complete prospective and attractive strategic transactions for many reasons, including competition from other potential acquirers, the effects of consolidation in our industry, and potentially high valuations of acquisition candidates. Even if we identify strategic transactions or enter into agreements with respect to such transactions, applicable antitrust laws and other regulations may limit our ability to acquire targets or force us to divest all or a portion of our business or an acquired business. If we are unable to identify suitable targets or complete acquisitions, our growth prospects may suffer, and we may not be able to realize sufficient scale and technological advantages to compete effectively.
Acquisitions involve many risks, any of which could adversely affect our business, financial condition, and results of operations, including:
•intense competition for suitable acquisition targets, which could increase prices and adversely affect our ability to consummate deals on favorable or acceptable terms;
•failure or material delay in closing a transaction;
•transaction-related lawsuits or claims;
•difficulties in integrating the technologies, operations, existing contracts, and personnel of an acquired company;
•difficulties in retaining key employees, business partners, or customers of an acquired company;
•challenges with integrating the brand identity of an acquired company with our own;
•diversion of financial and management resources from existing operations or alternative acquisition opportunities;
•failure to realize the anticipated benefits or synergies of a transaction at all or within the expected timeframe;
•the incurrence of debt or dilution related to equity issuances in connection with a transaction;

•failure to identify or address the problems, liabilities, or other shortcomings or challenges of an acquired company or technology, including issues related to copyrights and other intellectual property, regulatory compliance practices, litigation, revenue recognition, or other accounting practices, or employee or user issues;
•risks that the acquired company could have a negative impact on our company culture;
•risks that regulatory bodies may enact new laws or promulgate new regulations that are adverse to an acquired company or business;
•risks that regulatory bodies do not approve our acquisitions or business combinations or delay such approvals;
•theft of our trade secrets or confidential information that we share with potential acquisition candidates;
•risk that an acquired company or investment in new services cannibalizes a portion of our existing business; and
•adverse market reaction to an acquisition.
If we fail to address the foregoing risks or other problems encountered in connection with past or future acquisitions of businesses, new technologies, services, and other assets, strategic investments, and other strategic transactions, or if we otherwise fail to realize the expected benefits of such acquisitions or investments, our business, financial condition, and results of operations could be adversely affected.

If we are unable to ensure that our models, platform, and applications interoperate with a variety of software applications that are developed by others, including our integration partners, we may become less competitive and our business, financial condition, and results of operations may be adversely affected.
Our models, platform, and applications must integrate with a variety of hardware and software platforms, and we need to continuously modify and enhance our models, platform, and applications to adapt to changes in hardware, software, and browser technologies. In particular, we have developed our models, platform, and applications to be able to easily integrate with third-party applications, including the applications of software providers that compete with us as well as our partners, through the interaction of APIs. In general, we rely on the providers of such software systems to allow us access to their APIs to enable these integrations. We are typically subject to standard terms and conditions of such providers, which govern the distribution, operation, and fees of such software systems, and which are subject to change by such providers from time to time. Our business will be harmed if any provider of such software systems:
•discontinues or limits our access to its software or APIs;
•modifies its terms of service or other policies, including fees charged to, or other restrictions on, us or other software developers;
•changes how information is accessed by us or our customers;
•establishes more favorable relationships with one or more of our competitors; or
•develops or otherwise favors its own competitive offerings over our models, platform, and applications.

To continue to grow our business, we will need to continue to expand internationally, and our non-U.S. operations and sales to customers outside of the United States expose us to risks inherent in international expansion, sales, and operations.
We are a U.S.-headquartered company with extensive U.S. operations, as well as subsidiaries and operations in several other countries and regions, including the United Kingdom, the EU, Canada, and Asia-Pacific, and we expect that our future growth will depend in part on expanding our operations, customer base, and market presence in additional jurisdictions. Operating in non-U.S. markets requires significant resources and management attention and subjects us to regulatory, economic, and political risks that are different from those in the United States. As we continue to expand internationally, we have faced and will continue to face risks in doing business internationally that could adversely affect our business, including:
•the need to localize and adapt our models, platform, and applications for specific countries, including translation into foreign languages and associated expenses;
•privacy laws, export controls, sanctions, and other national security regulations that impose different and potentially conflicting obligations with respect to how personal data is processed or require that customer data be stored in a designated territory;
•laws in certain jurisdictions that may compel our personnel, vendors, or facility operators to provide governmental authorities with access to our systems, data, or other information, in some cases without notice to us and with limited means of recourse;
•national security-driven data security regulations. For example, the Department of Justice issued a final rule, effective in April 2025, that places limitations, and in some cases prohibitions, on certain transfers of covered data to partners, service providers, vendors, employees, and investors located in China or with other specified links to China (and other designated countries). We may be required to re-examine current contracts or engage in new contract negotiations with third parties that aid in processing data on our behalf or otherwise have access to covered data. These rules also may broadly require us to extract promises from other third-party service providers that they will not transfer data we share with them onward to parties linked to countries of concern;
•import regulations, including U.S. and non-U.S. governments’ restrictive trade policies, customs duties and other parallel regulations, tariffs, and countermeasures;

•AI, data-related, cybersecurity, and digital service laws and regulations (such as the EU AI Act, NIS2, the EU Digital Services Act, the United Kingdom’s Online Safety Act 2023, and the EU Data Act) that are rapidly evolving, require continuous monitoring to maintain compliance, and often provide for substantial penalties in the event of a violation;
•difficulties in staffing and managing non-U.S. operations, including the complexity of engaging in various employer-of-record arrangements that are geographically dispersed, and the risk that these arrangements, or any failure to establish local employing entities on a timely basis, may not comply with local employment, labor, or tax laws, exposing us to fines, penalties, reclassification, or back-payment claims;
•difficulties of maintaining our culture across various jurisdictions;
•regulatory and other delays and difficulties in setting up non-U.S. operations, including delays in forming, registering, and obtaining governmental approvals for local subsidiaries, delays in establishing local banking, payroll, and benefits arrangements, and difficulties relating to safety of personnel and information, any of which could delay local hiring efforts;
•the potential need for reviews by the U.S. government or non-U.S. governments of certain transactions with non-U.S. entities;
•different pricing environments, longer sales cycles, longer accounts receivable payment cycles, and collections issues;
•new and different sources of competition;
•weaker protection for, and practical difficulties in enforcing, intellectual property and other legal rights outside of the United States compared to protections available domestically;
•laws and business practices favoring local competitors;
•compliance challenges related to the complexity of multiple, conflicting, and changing governmental laws and regulations, including copyright, employment, tax, privacy, and data protection laws and regulations, export controls, and sanctions;
•parties outside the United States seeking to circumvent sanctions and export controls;
•increased financial accounting and reporting burdens and complexities;
•increased costs in establishing infrastructure across various jurisdictions;
•declines in the values of foreign currencies relative to the U.S. dollar;
•restrictions on the transfer of funds;
•potentially adverse tax consequences;
•the cost of and potential outcomes of any claims or litigation;
•future accounting pronouncements and changes in accounting policies;
•changes in tax laws or tax regulations; and
•regional and local economic and political conditions.

