•prior to the Class F Sunset Time and the Class T Sunset Time, stockholders may act by written consent only with respect to (i) any action to nominate, elect, or remove directors or appoint a director to fill any vacancy or newly created directorship; (ii) any action to satisfy certain redemption and protective provisions in our amended and restated certificate of incorporation related to our Class F common stock, Class T common stock, and Class E common stock; and (iii) any matter in which stockholders vote together as a single class if such matter is first approved or requested by our board of directors, and following both the Class F Sunset Time and the Class T Sunset Time, any action required or permitted to be taken by our stockholders must be effected at a duly called annual or special meeting of stockholders and may not be effected by any written consent of our stockholders; notwithstanding the foregoing, any action required or permitted to be taken by the holders of our Class E common stock must be effected at a duly called annual or special meeting of stockholders and may not be effected by any consent in writing by such stockholders;
•advance notice procedures required for Class A stockholders to nominate candidates for election as directors or to bring matters before an annual meeting of stockholders;
•following the Class F Sunset Time, our board of directors will shift to having a classified board of directors consisting of three classes of directors with staggered three-year terms;
•following the Class F Sunset Time, 66 2/3% of the voting power of our Class A common stock will be required to amend certain provisions in our amended and restated certificate of incorporation and our amended and restated bylaws;
•not providing for cumulative voting; and
•authorizing undesignated preferred stock, the issuance and terms of which may be established by 80% of the authorized members of our board of directors (rounded up or down to the nearest whole number (any exact 0.5 rounding up)) and, prior to the Class F Sunset Time, only with the consent of the Founder LLC as the holder of our Class F common stock.

The market price of our Class A common stock may be volatile, and you may lose all or part of your investment.
We cannot predict the prices at which our Class A common stock will trade. The initial public offering price of our Class A common stock will be determined through negotiations among us, the selling stockholders, and the underwriters. This price will not necessarily reflect the price at which investors in the market will be willing to buy and sell shares of our Class A common stock following this offering. The market price of our Class A common stock following this offering may fluctuate substantially, depending on a number of factors, many of which are beyond our control and may not be related to our operating performance. These fluctuations may cause you to lose all or part of your investment in our Class A common stock. Factors that may cause fluctuations in the market price of our Class A common stock include the following:
•price and volume fluctuations in the overall stock market from time to time, including fluctuations due to general economic uncertainty or negative market sentiment;
•volatility in the market and trading volumes of technology stocks;
•supply chain constraints, particularly those impacting compute, and new and evolving trade regulations and tariffs, and countermeasures;
•changes in the operating performance and stock market valuations of other technology companies generally, or those in our industry in particular;
•sales of shares of our Class A common stock by us or our stockholders, as well as the anticipation of lock-up releases;
•rumors and market speculation involving us or other companies in our industry or related industries;
•failure of securities analysts to maintain coverage of us, changes in financial estimates by securities analysts who follow our company, or our failure to meet these estimates or the expectations of investors;
•actual or perceived significant data breaches involving our business;
•the financial or non-financial projections we may provide to the public, any changes in those projections, or our failure to meet those projections;
•third-party data published about us or other companies in our industry, whether or not such data is accurate;
•announcements by us or our competitors of new models, platforms, or applications;
•the public’s reaction to our press releases, other public announcements, and filings with the SEC;

•fluctuations in the trading volume of shares of our Class A common stock or the size of our public float;
•short selling of our Class A common stock or related derivative securities;
•actual or anticipated changes or fluctuations in our results of operations;
•actual or anticipated developments in our business, our competitors’ businesses, or the competitive landscape generally;
•changes in the anticipated future size or growth rate of our addressable markets;
•our issuance of shares of our Class A common stock, including as a result of the conversion of our Class N common stock in connection with certain transfers;
•litigation or regulatory action involving us, our industry, or both, or investigations by regulators into our operations or those of our competitors;
•developments or disputes concerning our intellectual property or other proprietary rights;
•announced or completed acquisitions of businesses or technologies, or similar strategic transactions, by us or our competitors;
•new laws or regulations or new interpretations of existing laws or regulations applicable to our business;
•regulatory enforcement actions or other governmental efforts affecting our models, platform, and applications;
•changes in accounting standards, policies, guidelines, interpretations, or principles;
•major catastrophic events such as war, incidents of terrorism, pandemics, or responses to such events;
•any additions or departures of founders, board members, management, or key personnel;
•general economic conditions and slow or negative growth of our markets;
•potential allocation to retail investors of a minority of shares of our Class A common stock sold in this offering; and
•retail investor interest in our Class A common stock on an ongoing basis following this offering.
In addition, if the market for technology stocks or the stock market in general experiences a loss of investor confidence, the market price of our Class A common stock may decline for reasons unrelated to our business, financial condition, and results of operations. The market price of our Class A common stock may also decline in reaction to events that affect other companies in our industry even if these events do not directly affect us. Accordingly, we cannot assure you of the liquidity of any trading market, your ability to sell your shares of our Class A common stock when desired, or the prices that you may obtain for your shares of our Class A common stock.

