Common Stock Valuations
Prior to our initial public offering, given the absence of a public trading market of our Class A common stock, and in accordance with the American Institute of Certified Public Accountants Accounting and Valuation Guide, Valuation of Privately-Held-Company Equity Securities Issued as Compensation, our board of directors exercised reasonable judgment and considered numerous objective and subjective factors to determine the best estimate of the fair value of our common stock, including:
•contemporaneous third-party valuations of our common stock;
•the prices paid for common or redeemable convertible preferred stock sold to third-party investors by us and prices paid in secondary transactions, including any tender offers;
•the rights, preferences, and privileges of our redeemable convertible preferred stock relative to those of our common stock;
•actual and forecasted operating and financial performance;
•our stage of development;
•the industry outlook;
•the lack of marketability of our common stock;
•the likelihood of achieving a liquidity event, such as an initial public offering or a sale of our company, given prevailing market conditions;
•the history and nature of our business, industry trends, and competitive environment; and
•general economic outlook, including economic growth, inflation and unemployment, interest rate environment, and global economic trends.
Our board of directors determined the fair value of our common stock by first determining the enterprise value of our business and then allocating the value among the various classes of our securities to derive a per share value of our common stock. The enterprise value of our business was primarily estimated by reference to the closest round of equity financing to the date of the valuation.
In allocating the enterprise value of our business among the various classes of stock prior to this offering, we used a hybrid method based on a contingent claims analysis (“CCA”) and a common stock equivalent (“CSE”) method. The CCA is based on the Black-Scholes-Merton option pricing model, which models each class of stock as a call option with a unique claim on our assets. The CSE method presumes that all shares of redeemable convertible preferred stock convert into common stock based upon their conversion terms and other differences in the rights and preferences of the shares of redeemable convertible preferred stock are ignored.

We believe that Powerful AI, a highly capable and impactful form of frontier intelligence characterized by the following key attributes, will become widely available within a few years:
•cognitive capabilities matching or exceeding those of top human experts across most professional and scientific disciplines;
•the ability to navigate all interfaces available to a human working virtually, including text, audio, video, and direct computer control;
•the ability to reason through complex tasks autonomously over extended periods—lasting hours, days, or weeks—and seek clarification and feedback when needed;
•the ability to coordinate across multiple instances and collaborate with both humans and other systems; and
•the ability to interface with the physical world by controlling laboratory equipment, robotic systems, and other machines through digital connections.
If Powerful AI is directed at solving humanity’s hardest problems and the risks are managed responsibly, the potential is extraordinary. The possibilities include compressing decades of scientific progress into years; accelerating drug discovery, disease detection, and the delivery of personalized therapy; providing individually tailored education and expert financial and legal advice; advancing power grids and battery design for greater efficiency and reliability; and extending to the developing world capabilities that took wealthy nations generations to build.

Long-Term Investment Philosophy
Building at the frontier requires large-scale investments, and our financial model demonstrates that we can make those investments while generating increasingly attractive returns. This is evidenced when comparing the three months ended September 30, 2026 to the three months ended September 30, 2025:
•Revenue increased 1,127%;
•Compute and infrastructure expense increased 382%;
•Other operating expenses, excluding the Bartz settlement, increased 729%;
•Operating margin improved from (224)% to (3)%; and
•Adjusted Operating Margin improved from (93)% to 15%.
This combination of unprecedented revenue growth, compute efficiency, and operating efficiency driving this model gives us the confidence to keep investing at scale, in both the near term and the long term.
To support our research, model development, customer demand, and internal usage of Claude, we continue to invest at scale. As of and subsequent to September 30, 2026, we have entered into purchase commitments and lease agreements, including short-term commitments, as well as leases not yet commenced, with an aggregate committed spend of approximately $900.9 billion. These commitments include approximately $451.6 billion expected to be incurred before December 31, 2030, and substantially all of the remainder is expected to be incurred between 2031 and 2045. Approximately $70.7 billion of these commitments are due in the 12 months following September 30, 2026. See the section titled “Management’s Discussion and Analysis of Financial Condition and Results of Operations—Liquidity and Capital Resources—Contractual Obligations” for more information.
As we invest heavily towards the incredible opportunity ahead, our core advantages enable us to generate more from every dollar we invest in scaling our business. The momentum in our financial trajectory increases our conviction to continue investing to build and scale Powerful AI that is safe, beneficial, and understandable, and positions us well for long-term growth.

Building on this approach, we have publicly called for “pacing the frontier,” by which we mean establishing industry-wide standards primarily related to the use of AI to accelerate AI development, so that AI capabilities do not advance faster than the safety, alignment, and security practices needed to support them. This call to pace the frontier contemplates frontier model developers collaborating with governments and does not describe our own model development or release timelines, which continue to be informed by our risk assessments. If standards of this kind are adopted industry-wide through government action or regulation, the rate at which AI capabilities advance could be faster or slower than it is today, depending on the extent to which safety, alignment, and security practices advance together with model capabilities. We believe the first step toward “pacing the frontier” is independent verification of compliance with future regulatory standards, and we have begun implementing a program under which independent evaluators work alongside our own risk and safety teams with broad internal access, subject to legal, confidentiality, and security limits. As part of this program, we and Accenture each currently anticipate investing at least $1 billion over the next five years to build external reviewer capacity. The terms of this arrangement have not been finalized and are subject to change. We have also called on other frontier model developers to adopt comparable practices to demonstrate that safety commitments in our industry can be independently verified. We have further supported regulation and oversight at the frontier, including measures focused on transparency and independent third-party auditing, and international coordination, so these practices apply across frontier developers rather than depend on voluntary adoption alone.
We believe frontier intelligence companies and governments should take action to strengthen the security of critical infrastructure against AI-related and other cyber threats, including by:
•providing responsible model access, funding, training, and hands-on support, particularly for under-resourced critical-infrastructure defenders;
•building observability and security tools;
•working to ensure agentic identities are traceable and accountable;
•sharing best practices in continuous monitoring;
•investing in authorized testing, private disclosure, and verified remediation of vulnerabilities; and
•sharing tools, playbooks, and threat assessments with governments, security partners, and open-source maintainers.

Status Quo
Beyond direct competition, one obstacle to the diffusion of Claude is the natural resistance to change within organizations. Many enterprises operate with deeply entrenched legacy systems, established workflows, and institutional processes that have been refined over many years. Adopting frontier capabilities such as Claude often requires substantial change management, including workforce retraining, process redesign, internal policy development, and shifts in corporate culture. These can introduce friction, cost, and perceived risk that can delay or prevent adoption. Especially in highly regulated or risk-averse industries—where the perceived downside risk of major changes can often outweigh the potential benefits—decision-makers might default to maintaining the status quo.
We believe that the principal competitive factors in our market include:
•Our ability to maintain our singularity of focus and mission as a company;
•Our ability to remain at and continue to expand the frontier of intelligence;
•Our ability to continue hiring and retaining world-class research talent;
•Our continued focus on safe, reliable, and trustworthy intelligence;
•Our access to compute necessary to train and deploy AI models, which may be subject to supply constraints, capacity limitations, or allocation decisions by third-party providers;
•Our ability to improve the efficiency with which our models are trained and deployed;
•Our ability to secure financing on acceptable terms;
•Our ability to integrate research, product, and our go-to-market approach to ensure we continue building models and applications that serve our customers and allow us to generate revenue to fund our investments in remaining at the frontier;
•Our ability to continue maintaining our partnerships across infrastructure providers and distribution partners;
•Our ability to attract and retain customers who are building their products on the Claude Platform;
•Our ability to attract new customers and retain and expand sales to existing customers;
•Our ability to continue building applications that anticipate the needs of the market; and •Our ability to navigate changes in the competitive landscape, including consolidation among competitors or customers and strategic partnerships between competitors.
We believe that we compete favorably across these factors and are uniquely positioned at the frontier of AI.

